WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.180
The three laws in genetics form the crux of this topic.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.740
These laws not only answered a few questions,

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.040
but were even ahead of time!

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.340
They were instrumental in proving a few important

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.200
concepts which were discovered in the following

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:20.920
years!

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.120
But do all the organisms always follow these

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.519
fundamental laws?

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.975
When crossed, do all organisms always give

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.380
offsprings in the ratios that Mendel obtained?

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.360
Not really!

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.740
Even this has exceptions!

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.080
But before we get to know the exceptions to

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.760
these laws, let us first try answering some

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.794
interesting questions that will help us understand

00:00:46.794 --> 00:00:49.160
the concepts learned so far thoroughly!

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.280
To begin with, let us imagine we have this

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.380
plant with axial flowers.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.440
We know that the axial flower position trait

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.040
is dominant over the recessive trait of the

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.780
terminal flower position!

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.240
So this plant has the dominant genes for the

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.200
character “flower position”!

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.400
Now this is the phenotype of the plant, which

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.880
is understood by a mere glance at it!

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.120
But can you tell me what the genotype of this plant is?

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.600
Is it homozygous or heterozygous for this character?

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.499
Let me simplify this for you.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.060
Let us consider the alleles “A” in capital

00:01:29.065 --> 00:01:33.140
to represent the dominant trait “axial position”

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.480
and “a” in lower case for the recessive

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.380
trait “terminal position”.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.880
So can you tell me whether this plant has

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.480
the genotype “AA” or “Aa”?

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.859
Just a glance is not enough.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:46.859
It could be either!

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.017
Now I am going to ask you an interesting question

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.480
and I want you to really try your best to

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.280
answer it!

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.220
Is there any way in which you can find the

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.380
genotype here?

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.120
Think about it.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.780
Maybe a test would help?

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.780
Can we look at the chromosomes and say which

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.400
genes are present on them?

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.340
Nope, that’s not possible at all!

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.610
Then what kind of a testing method will help us?

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.220
It’s simple actually!

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.832
The only way to know the genotype of this

00:02:18.832 --> 00:02:21.720
plant is by crossing this with a plant whose

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.860
genotype is known to us!

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.800
So what plant will we opt for in order to cross?

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.020
The best choice will be a plant that is RECESSIVE

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.110
in phenotype.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.280
In this case we will opt for a plant with

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.320
terminal flowers!

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.060
But why should we take a plant with a recessive trait?

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.580
Why not dominant?

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.020
Because for a recessive plant, it will definitely

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.260
have the alleles “aa”, BOTH in lower case.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.079
This is the genotype we can be sure about.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.380
This is the unknown genotype for us!

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.260
After crossing, the F one generation will

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.120
get one allele “a” written in lower case

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.400
from this parent.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.360
So we write these “a” in all these four places.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.280
Talking about the genotype of our plant, there

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.840
could be two possibilities.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.120
One, it could be ‘AA’ both capital.

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:20.679
Or it could be “Aa’ one in capital and

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.290
the other in lower case!

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.000
These are the two possibilities or cases!

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.780
Let us look at case 1.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.500
On crossing, in the F1 generation we obtain

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.580
all the plants which have axial flower positions.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.220
This means all the plants will be heterozygous

00:03:39.227 --> 00:03:42.310
with one “capital A” and one lower case

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:42.940
“a”.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.600
Now we move on to case 2.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.400
Look at the plants obtained in the F one generation.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.620
We got 2 axial, and 2 terminal.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.480
They are in the ratio ‘one to one’.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.140
What can we deduce from these 2 cases?

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.600
Simple…

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.960
We can guess the unknown genotype by looking

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.260
at the results in the F one generation!

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.760
If we cross our plant with a plant having

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.780
a recessive phenotype, AND we get all plants

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.727
with flowers in axial positions, then the

00:04:15.727 --> 00:04:18.531
genotype of our plant will be ‘AA’ both

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.531
in capital.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.040
And if we get plants with dominant and recessive

00:04:23.048 --> 00:04:26.639
traits in the ratio one to one, then the genotype

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:29.887
of our plant will be ‘Aa’ one in capital

00:04:29.887 --> 00:04:32.160
and the other in lower case.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.590
Wasn’t this interesting?

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:40.340
We used another plant to find the genotype of a plant.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.010
This type of a cross is referred to as a “TEST CROSS”.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:49.540
It is a cross where the individual’s zygosity is found.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.760
Zygosity simply means the homozygous or

00:04:51.764 --> 00:04:53.880
heterozygous nature.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.120
It is found by crossing it with a recessive individual.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.060
Test crosses are the most powerful tools used

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.800
in several experiments.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.900
Let us try to solve another question in the next part!

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:16.829
Phenotype and genotype are the important concepts

00:05:16.829 --> 00:05:18.680
which we know pretty well!

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.760
Can you tell me how many phenotypes are obtained

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.860
on crossing a homozygous plant having Round

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.314
and yellow seeds with another homozygous plant

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.710
having wrinkled and green seeds?

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.020
We want the NUMBER of phenotypes and not the ratio!

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.200
Let us crack this together!

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.780
The first thing we need to note is that we

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.020
are looking at two characters.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.240
Seed shape and seed colour.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.560
We are crossing this plant with the one having

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.759
wrinkled and green seeds.

00:05:52.759 --> 00:05:55.610
So isn’t this a Dihybrid cross?

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:56.940
Yes it is!

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.422
Now let’s get to the next step!

00:05:59.422 --> 00:06:01.400
We are supposed to calculate the number of

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.560
phenotypes obtained.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.140
On crossing these, the F1 generation as we

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.580
know, will always have all the dominant characters

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:10.600
expressed.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.380
This is because the F1 hybrids are all heterozygous

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:16.440
in nature.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.520
So there’s just one phenotype the F 1 generation

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.920
that is ‘round and yellow’ in this case.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:25.980
What about the number of phenotypes in the

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:26.980
F two generation?

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.640
If you remember the table of any dihybrid

00:06:30.643 --> 00:06:34.569
cross, we obtain the phenotypic ratio 9 to

00:06:34.569 --> 00:06:36.320
3 to 3 to 1.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.280
Take a moment to go through this table that

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.480
we’d seen previously!

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.300
What will be the number of phenotypes in the

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.000
F two generation?

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.580
As there are FOUR different varieties of plants,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:52.500
we can say that there are four phenotypes.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.676
You should know that a Dihybrid cross always

00:06:55.676 --> 00:06:58.430
gives us four different phenotypes in the

00:06:58.430 --> 00:06:59.420
F2 generation!

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.940
Now let us make it more interesting!

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:07.000
Can you tell me what the genotypic ratio obtained

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.520
in the F two generation after a Dihybrid cross?

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.440
9 to 3 to 3 to 1 is the phenotypic ratio.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:20.900
You can pause the video and try finding the
genotypic ratio of these offsprings!

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.759
The answer is tricky!

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.440
The genotypic ratio obtained for the F2

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.979
generation is this!

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:33.330
Huge isn’t it?

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.100
Why is it so big?

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.429
It’s because there are 9 different genotypes

00:07:38.429 --> 00:07:40.160
according to the table!

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.300
Study of genetics is indeed very interesting!

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.837
Now can you answer one more question that

00:07:47.837 --> 00:07:49.300
Nora has for you?

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:52.457
She wants to know which cross or crosses explain

00:07:52.457 --> 00:07:54.759
the Law of Independent assortment ?

00:07:54.759 --> 00:07:59.580
Think about the law first!

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.365
It states that, “Genes for different traits

00:08:02.365 --> 00:08:04.440
can segregate independently during gamete

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:05.680
formation.”

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:09.860
Now if we consider a monohybrid cross, then

00:08:09.862 --> 00:08:12.610
the event of assortment of genes is ruled

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:16.080
out as there is only one pair considered at a time!

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.120
However, when there are two or more genes

00:08:19.125 --> 00:08:22.169
involved, as we can see in this case, the

00:08:22.169 --> 00:08:24.480
dominant and the recessive alleles of the

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.320
different genes assort independent of each

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:27.940
other!

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.940
That is the reason why we get four different

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.480
combinations at the end!

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.920
Thus this law is best understood when we study

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.400
DIHYBRID or higher crosses which involve MORE

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.400
characters.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.920
‘d’ is the best answer!

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.374
Well, we have been talking about Dihybrid

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.700
crosses and Dihybrid ratios since quite some

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:52.360
time.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.200
Let’s try cracking just one simple question

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.960
related to a Trihybrid cross in the next part!

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.480
We have been talking about Monohybrid and

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.060
Dihybrid crosses for quite a while.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:12.932
Now let us try answering a question related

00:09:12.932 --> 00:09:14.330
to a Trihybrid cross!

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:16.420
It’s very simple!

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.940
Let’s say, we consider the three characters

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.810
like the height of the plant, seed shape and

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.130
seed colour, all being genotypically heterozygous!

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.905
Now can you guess how many possibilities or

00:09:29.905 --> 00:09:32.680
combinations of gametes will be formed from

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:33.680
these?

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.980
Will there be four possibilities, or six,

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:38.750
or eight combinations?

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.233
You can pause the video and make the necessary

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.050
calculations on a paper!

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.680
We get eight possibilities when a heterozygous

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.640
individual for all the three traits is considered.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:57.240
The simple trick is by using the formula 2 raised to n.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.277
Here, “n” is the number of characters

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.000
considered at a time.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.100
Now since this is a Trihybrid cross, the formula

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:10.480
will be 2 to the power 3, which gives us 8!

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.580
So there are 8 possible combinations of gametes

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.160
that are obtained.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.640
Now to take it a step ahead, can you tell

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.110
me the different combinations of genes in

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:21.700
these eight gametes?

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:26.240
Let’s solve this together!

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:30.860
The genotype of this parent is “TtRrYy”

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:33.779
all letters written in one upper and one lower

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:36.840
case to represent heterozygous genotype.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.758
To get the gametes, we take the help of the

00:10:40.758 --> 00:10:43.610
Law of independent assortment.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.280
According to the law, the alleles for different

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.900
genes will assort independent of each other.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:52.378
So in simple words, the alleles “T” and

00:10:52.378 --> 00:10:55.269
“t” will assort independent of the other

00:10:55.269 --> 00:11:00.190
alleles “R” and “r” and even “Y” and “y”.

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:02.820
That means this “T” can get assorted with

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.420
either “R” or “r” and similarly with

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.300
either “Y” or “y”.

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:11.460
There’s a simple way to find the combinations!

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.320
We write the letters “capital T” and “lower

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.420
case t” four times each.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.680
Why do we write it four times?

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.220
That is because we know that each allele has

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.860
the possibility of assorting with all the

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:27.540
remaining four alleles.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.820
So we write the letters “T” and “t” like this!

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:34.740
Now the second set of genes can be assorted

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.120
in an alternate fashion written twice.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.560
So we write capital “R” here and here,

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.320
followed by “r” in lower case twice.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.440
We repeat the same procedure with the “t”

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.140
allele written in lower case.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:52.100
Now comes the last allele set!

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.400
Any idea how will this be assorted?

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:56.940
Well, it’s quite simple!

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:02.100
The last set will be written in single alternate fashion.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:06.020
That is “Y” in capital followed by “y” in lower case!

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:11.880
This is done one after the other to find all the gametes.

00:12:11.889 --> 00:12:14.868
So a total of 8 gametes, or combinations to

00:12:14.868 --> 00:12:17.709
be precise, are obtained in an individual

00:12:17.709 --> 00:12:21.020
that is heterozygous for all the three traits!

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:25.480
Now let me ask you an extremely interesting question!

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.855
If we cross a plant having red flowers with

00:12:28.855 --> 00:12:32.230
a plant having white coloured flowers, then

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:35.140
is there a possibility to obtain an intermediate

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.080
flower colour like pink?

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.320
At the first instance you may say NO!

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.640
Most of us would think that the plants in

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.199
the F1 generation can either be red or white

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.340
depending upon the dominant trait!

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:53.780
But what if I tell you that there are certain

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:56.280
exceptional plants which show this interesting

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:57.280
characteristic?

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.500
Want to know about these exceptions?

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.740
For that, we will have to understand the exceptions

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.500
to Mendelian inheritance!

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.320
Meet you in the next part

